+# Daily Grind account UI
+
+The account page is at `/` and the original coffee storefront is preserved at
+`/home.html`. Its My Account link opens the account page. This repository has no
+authentication service or database, so the UI defaults to an explicitly labeled
+demo account. Demo renames and removals persist in localStorage, falling back to
+memory if storage is blocked. Demo credentials never reach the WebAuthn Signal API.
+
+## Connecting the customer account backend
+
+Before the account module runs, supply this configuration from the authenticated
+server-rendered page (use your framework's safe JSON serialization):
+
+```html
+<script>
+  window.DAILY_GRIND_ACCOUNT = {
+    apiBase: '/api/passkeys',
+    csrfToken: 'SERVER_GENERATED_SESSION_CSRF_TOKEN'
+  };
+</script>
+```
+
+The same-origin, cookie-authenticated API must implement:
+
+| Request | Behavior |
+| --- | --- |
+| `GET /api/passkeys` | Return the signed-in customer's complete credential snapshot. |
+| `PUT /api/passkeys/:id` | Validate `{ "name": "…" }`, update only that customer's credential nickname, and return the complete updated snapshot. |
+| `DELETE /api/passkeys/:id` | Delete only that customer's credential and return the complete updated snapshot. |
+
+Every successful response must use this shape:
+
+```json
+{
+  "user": {
+    "id": "BASE64URL_WEBAUTHN_USER_HANDLE",
+    "displayName": "Alex Morgan",
+    "name": "alex@example.com"
+  },
+  "rpId": "YOUR_REGISTERED_RP_DOMAIN",
+  "credentials": [
+    {
+      "id": "BASE64URL_CREDENTIAL_ID",
+      "name": "My laptop",
+      "aaguid": "fbfc3007-154e-4ecc-8c0b-6e020557d7bd",
+      "provider": "Apple Passwords",
+      "kind": "laptop",
+      "registeredAt": 1768348800000,
+      "lastUsedAt": null
+    }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+Replace the example IDs and domain with the original WebAuthn registration values.
+Dates are epoch milliseconds; `lastUsedAt` can be null. `kind` is an optional visual
+hint (`laptop`, `phone`, or `usb`); an unknown value shows a generic key icon.
+Known AAGUIDs supply provider names; unrecognized or zeroed AAGUIDs fall back to
+the optional provider label or “Unknown passkey provider”. These are display hints.
+
+The server must authenticate every request, enforce credential ownership and CSRF
+protection on mutations, validate name length (1–50 after trimming), and prevent
+account lockout according to its recovery policy. It should return 401 for an
+expired session and 403 for failed CSRF checks. Do not accept user identity from
+request parameters. Return errors as non-2xx responses and snapshots with
+`Cache-Control: no-store`. The client sends `X-CSRF-Token` on mutations.
+
+**Always return all credential IDs for this user and RP, including credentials on
+other devices.** Never paginate or filter this response. An incomplete accepted
+list can cause an authenticator to remove valid credentials. An empty list is only
+valid if the signed-in customer really has no remaining passkeys. Do not include
+public key material or other customers' credentials.
+
+For live accounts, the client feature-detects `signalAllAcceptedCredentials` and
+signals the complete validated list on account load and after confirmed deletion.
+Nickname changes only update the account record; they do not call
+`signalCurrentUserDetails`. Unsupported browsers get manual cleanup instructions;
+failed signals preserve the successful server change and offer a retry that fetches
+a fresh snapshot. Failed API responses never fall back to demo credentials.
+
+No passkey creation, authentication, account profile mutation, or real sign-out is
+implemented by this static UI. Wire those into the existing account service when
+integrating it. The supplemental orders/rewards views are placeholders.
+
+## Checks
+
+No development/preview server or browser verification was used. JavaScript syntax
+was checked with `node --check account.js` and `node --check passkeys.mjs`, and
+patch whitespace with `git diff --check`. This static project needs no build.
